items.content:
@BicycleSocials They should have put the resignation in their pockets and said, "Okay, we're not accepting your resignation today, but we're going to put you on unpaid leave for a few months until all these questions are cleared up, and then we'll decide." 

Instead, they got their emotions involved and created an even bigger mess for themselves.

And really, for all of us, because this company is really all about American competitiveness, and that has seen real harm the past two weeks.

@KeyTryer gotta be the most empty-ass reply I have seen.

literally a declaration of affiliation without a trace of an argument.

presumably because you don’t have one.

@Sr_Lazarus Of course it's not okay to break the rules, but he built this company. Nobody else did, so he deserves a little bit of due process and a little bit of decorum. We don't even know all the facts yet. 

He should have been put on unpaid leave. That would have communicated to the markets that the board is not happy with him, and they could wait until all the facts are in and the investigations are done. Then come to the market and say, "Here's our decision based on the facts and the investigation." 

This whole thing is just the worst board firing I've seen. I can't think of one that's worse.

